        "bodyTr": "Industry Minister Kim Jung-kwan, who returned from China last week, posted on Facebook over the weekend with the title \"Are we desperate enough? Are we doing it?\" The post conveyed the sense of crisis and urgency he felt on the ground. He wrote, \"The technological capabilities and competitiveness of Chinese companies are rising far faster than we thought,\" and added, \"In fields where we once led, we now find ourselves chasing China instead. I was deeply worried about whether we can truly be okay continuing as we are.\" He also relayed the grievances of Korean business leaders in China, saying that while the entire Chinese nation mobilizes to allow companies to compete to their fullest potential, he questioned whether we are doing the same. Minister Kim stated, \"Shouldn't the government be even more desperate than businesses? If a company has to wait years to resolve a single regulation, how much further ahead will competing companies from other nations have gone in that time?\" Many citizens will likely resonate with his heartfelt appeal that \"at the very least, businesses should not lose opportunities or suffer in silence because of government obstacles.\"\n\nThe Ministry of Industry is attempting to eliminate unnecessary regulations and complicated permitting procedures across regional boundaries through the Ultra-Wide Special Zone Act, but progress has stalled due to opposition from the Ministry of Employment and Labor and labor groups. The Democratic Party has submitted two contradictory bills to the National Assembly: one containing labor exemptions that business circles have long demanded, including exceptions to the 52-hour workweek for high-earning professionals and extending the usage period for fixed-term workers from two to four years, and another opposing bill that excludes labor exemptions and reflects labor's positions. This is our reality: the ruling party in power is postponing responsible decisions on national affairs.\n\nChina's technological rise, which Minister Kim mentioned, is not a recent phenomenon. Bloomberg Intelligence stated in a recent report that China is expected to emerge as a global leader in eight of seventeen strategic technology fields by 2030, including large language models, humanoid robots, and autonomous and connected vehicles. If China seizes control of future industries ranging from artificial intelligence to shipbuilding, automobiles, and biotechnology, Korea's industrial standing will inevitably shrink proportionally. If the industry minister is not alone in sensing this crisis, the government and ruling party must quickly make a decision on labor exemptions. Merely watching social dialogue drag on indefinitely is itself an act of irresponsibility.",
